WEBVTT

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:05.690
A student working through methods for SNP data kept getting stuck on one question.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.680
To estimate relatedness, you seem to need to know how inbred the population is.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.200
To estimate inbreeding, you seem to need to know how related its members are.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.370
It looks like a chicken-and-egg problem.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:19.040
In this program we take it apart, and show that most of the circle is an illusion.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.560
Relatedness and inbreeding are one quantity, seen from two angles.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.820
The genuine dependency lies elsewhere, in the reference population:

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.570
its allele frequencies and its structure.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.400
And there are good tools for handling it.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.270
We build from first principles to the methods you will actually run, and when each one works.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.430
Watch the lexicon on the right of the screen.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.450
Every numbered term is collected in the companion study guide.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:44.610
Chapter one.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:49.650
Identity by descent: the single idea beneath both relatedness and inbreeding.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.510
Two alleles are identical by state when they carry the same sequence.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.405
They are identical by descent when both are copies of one ancestral allele,

00:00:59.405 --> 00:01:00.850
inherited through the pedigree.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.140
Follow one allele in a grandparent.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.052
Through Mendelian segregation,

00:01:05.052 --> 00:01:08.480
a copy passes to each of two children, and from them to two cousins.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.440
The cousins now carry alleles that are identical by descent.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.779
Identity by state is what we observe, and at a marker

00:01:14.779 --> 00:01:17.270
with two alleles it happens by chance all the time.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:21.070
Two strangers can carry the same letter for entirely different ancestral reasons.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.350
Every method in this program does the same basic job.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.841
It infers identity by descent from observed identity by state,

00:01:27.841 --> 00:01:31.380
after subtracting the chance matching expected from allele frequencies.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.102
The kinship coefficient, φ, is the probability that an allele drawn at random from individual i,

00:01:39.102 --> 00:01:42.840
and one drawn at the same locus from individual j, are identical by descent.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:44.950
Take a parent and its offspring.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.250
Half the time, the allele drawn from the child is the one this parent gave it.

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:51.700
Half the time after that, the draw from the parent picks that same allele.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.360
So φ = ¼.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:56.910
Full siblings share both parents, and their kinship is also one quarter.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.410
Half siblings share one parent, with kinship one eighth.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.580
First cousins have kinship one sixteenth.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:06.293
In general, kinship sums over every path through a common ancestor

00:02:06.293 --> 00:02:10.977
A. A path through n individuals, counting i and j, contributes (½)ⁿ (1 + F_A),

00:02:10.977 --> 00:02:14.040
where F_A is the inbreeding of the ancestor itself.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.854
The inbreeding coefficient, F, is the probability

00:02:18.854 --> 00:02:22.450
that the two alleles within a single individual are identical by descent.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.600
Where did those two alleles come from?

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.710
One from the mother, and one from the father.

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.220
So an individual's F is exactly the kinship of its parents.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.140
Inbreeding is relatedness, one generation back.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.930
Here, two half siblings mate, closing a loop in the pedigree.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.330
Their kinship is one eighth, so their offspring is inbred, with F equal to one eighth.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.540
Now draw twice from one individual, with replacement.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.680
Half the time you pick the same allele twice.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.039
Otherwise you pick both, and they match by descent

00:02:50.039 --> 00:02:53.650
with probability F. That gives the self-kinship, (1 + F) / 2.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.170
So arrange every kinship in one matrix.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:00.260
Pairwise kinship fills the off-diagonal, and inbreeding sits on the diagonal.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.340
Both come from the same genotypes, estimated together.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.480
You never need one in order to get the other.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.940
Relatedness, r, is the scale most ecologists report.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:16.593
For outbred individuals it is simply twice the kinship: one half for parent and offspring

00:03:16.593 --> 00:03:20.560
and for full siblings, one quarter for half siblings, one eighth for first cousins.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.970
Those tidy values hold only when nobody is inbred.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.977
Wright's definition allows for inbreeding: r equals 2φ,

00:03:27.977 --> 00:03:31.190
divided by the square root of (1 + F_i)(1 + F_j).

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.256
If the parents of a sibship are themselves related,

00:03:34.256 --> 00:03:36.390
the siblings share more than the textbook half.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:40.620
A correct estimator should report relatedness above one half, and that is not a bias.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.431
Much of the confusion behind our puzzle comes from

00:03:43.431 --> 00:03:45.450
treating one half and one quarter as fixed facts.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:49.200
They are expectations for one special case: an outbred pedigree.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:52.880
Chapter two.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.990
The reference population: identity by descent, relative to what?

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:02.930
Go back far enough and every pair of individuals shares an ancestor.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:07.570
Given enough generations, every allele in a population is identical by descent with every other.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:11.337
So identity by descent only has meaning relative to a base population:

00:04:11.337 --> 00:04:14.030
a point in the past where we declare everyone unrelated.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:17.750
Pedigree coefficients set that base at the founders of the pedigree.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:22.057
Marker-based estimators set it implicitly, through the allele frequencies you supply,

00:04:22.057 --> 00:04:24.310
which are usually estimated from your own sample.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:26.690
That makes marker estimates relative.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.272
They ask whether a pair is more related than the average pair in your sample,

00:04:30.272 --> 00:04:31.140
which is set to zero.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:34.930
Pairs less related than average receive negative values, and that is not an error.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.270
This is where the real dependency lives.

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.319
Not between relatedness and inbreeding,

00:04:39.319 --> 00:04:41.940
but between both of them and the reference allele frequencies.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.650
Change the reference, and every estimate shifts together.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:51.170
How do estimators turn identity by state into identity by descent?

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:55.470
Sharing a common allele is weak evidence, because almost any two individuals would share it.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:57.780
Sharing a rare allele is strong evidence.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.340
Moment estimators weight every locus by its allele frequency.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:06.402
The genomic relationship matrix is the clearest example: center each genotype by twice the

00:05:06.402 --> 00:05:11.220
allele frequency, scale by its expected variance, and average the cross-products across loci.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.831
The Queller and Goodnight, Lynch and Ritland,

00:05:13.831 --> 00:05:16.520
and Wang estimators follow the same logic with different weights.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.837
They differ in bias and variance, especially when loci are few

00:05:19.837 --> 00:05:22.660
or individuals are inbred, so compare them on your own data.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.370
Their shared weak point is the allele frequency.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:28.302
If it is wrong, perhaps estimated from a small sample

00:05:28.302 --> 00:05:31.550
or from a mixture of populations, every estimate inherits the error.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.150
Which brings us to structure.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:38.515
Suppose your sample spans two genetically differentiated populations,

00:05:38.515 --> 00:05:41.050
and you estimate allele frequencies from the pooled sample.

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:44.660
An allele that is common in one population looks much less common overall.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:48.831
Now two unrelated individuals from the same population share that allele,

00:05:48.831 --> 00:05:50.940
and the estimator reads the match as kinship.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:55.550
Pairs within a population look related, and pairs between populations look negatively related.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:59.285
The same mixture also produces a deficit of heterozygotes,

00:05:59.285 --> 00:06:01.550
the Wahlund effect, which mimics inbreeding.

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:03.910
Structure inflates both quantities at once.

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.630
That is the true chicken and egg.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:07.290
There are two remedies.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.911
Analyze each genetic cluster separately,

00:06:09.911 --> 00:06:13.630
after identifying clusters with PCA, ADMIXTURE, or the sNMF method.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:16.170
Or use estimators that are robust to structure.

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:19.770
KING-robust compares a pair without using sample allele frequencies at all.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.160
PC-AiR and PC-Relate resolve the loop explicitly.

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.420
KING finds a set of unrelated individuals.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:28.160
Principal components from that set define ancestry.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.981
PC-Relate then estimates kinship and inbreeding using

00:06:30.981 --> 00:06:33.630
allele frequencies specific to each individual's ancestry.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:35.430
The circle becomes an iteration.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:39.230
Chapter three.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:43.820
Reading relationships: what a pair's genotypes can tell you, and what they cannot.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:50.770
At any locus, an outbred pair shares zero, one, or two alleles identical by descent.

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:56.390
Cotterman's coefficients, k0, k1 and k2, give the probability of each, and they sum to one.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:00.570
Parent and offspring always share exactly one: k1 equals one.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:05.420
Full siblings share zero, one or two, with probabilities one quarter, one half, and one quarter.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:09.880
Both relationships have kinship one quarter, but they sit at different points in this space.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:15.270
Kinship is a line through the space: φ = k1/4 + k2/2.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:18.599
Relationship-inference methods use the position along that line,

00:07:18.599 --> 00:07:20.110
not just which line a pair is on.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.852
Half siblings, an aunt and her nephew, and a grandparent

00:07:23.852 --> 00:07:26.630
and grandchild all have k0 and k1 equal to one half.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:28.180
They occupy the same point.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:31.143
No method using unlinked markers can separate them;

00:07:31.143 --> 00:07:34.310
that takes linkage information, or ancillary data such as ages.

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.900
Unrelated pairs sit in the corner, where k0 equals one.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:41.790
Real estimates scatter around each point, because every locus is a noisy draw.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:46.840
Cotterman's space assumes that neither individual is inbred.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:51.290
Allow inbreeding, and each individual's own two alleles may also be identical by descent.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:56.539
Among four alleles, two in each individual, there are fifteen patterns of identity,

00:07:56.539 --> 00:08:01.960
which collapse to nine distinguishable states: Jacquard's condensed identity coefficients, Δ1 to Δ9.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:07.560
For an outbred pair, only the last three remain, and they are Cotterman's k2, k1 and k0.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:15.960
Kinship is still perfectly well defined: φ = Δ1 + ½(Δ3 + Δ5 + Δ7) + ¼Δ8.

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:19.660
What breaks is the mapping from estimated coefficients to named relationships.

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:23.770
Force an inbred pair into the three-coefficient model and it lands in the wrong place.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:28.170
Inbred half siblings can share two alleles by descent, drifting toward full siblings,

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:30.900
and an inbred parent and offspring slide off their corner.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:35.370
Misclassification is the main danger, and three-coefficient kinship can be biased too.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:38.480
Pairwise estimators can be built on all nine states.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.917
NgsRelate version two estimates them from genotype likelihoods,

00:08:41.917 --> 00:08:45.340
and the related package allows inbreeding in its likelihood estimators.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:50.390
Linked markers carry more information than their count suggests.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:56.033
Identity by descent arrives in segments: stretches of chromosome inherited intact from a shared ancestor,

00:08:56.033 --> 00:08:58.530
and cut shorter by recombination every generation.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.780
A grandparent and grandchild share long segments, cut by a single meiosis.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:07.290
Half siblings' segments pass through two meioses, an aunt and nephew's through three.

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:10.875
The same expected sharing arrives in different lengths and numbers of pieces,

00:09:10.875 --> 00:09:13.730
and that is how linkage-aware methods tell these relationships apart.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:16.570
Segments also reveal realized relatedness.

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:21.142
Full siblings are expected to share one half, but Mendelian sampling is random,

00:09:21.142 --> 00:09:24.575
so the actual fraction varies, roughly from 0.4 to 0.6 in humans,

00:09:24.575 --> 00:09:29.540
and more widely in species with shorter genetic maps, meaning fewer chromosomes or crossovers.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.190
Genomic estimates measure what was actually inherited.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.962
Using segments requires a reference genome,

00:09:34.962 --> 00:09:37.830
ideally assembled to chromosomes, and dense markers along it.

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:42.200
A panel of a few hundred amplicons cannot see segments; whole-genome data can.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.760
Many ecological questions never need a named relationship.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:52.422
Take sex-biased dispersal: if females stay near where they were born and males disperse,

00:09:52.422 --> 00:09:55.860
nearby females should be more related to one another than nearby males.

00:09:56.610 --> 00:10:00.105
Estimate kinship for every female–female, female–male, and male–male pair,

00:10:00.105 --> 00:10:03.600
and compare the classes, often as a function of the distance between them.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:08.669
Any bias that shifts the baseline, from inbreeding or from imperfect allele frequencies,

00:10:08.669 --> 00:10:12.840
shifts all the classes roughly together, as long as the sexes share one ancestry mix.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:16.920
The question concerns the difference between classes, and the shared offset cancels.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.200
Test the difference by permutation.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:24.020
Shuffle the sex labels, recompute, and ask how often a difference this large arises by chance.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:26.206
Spatial autocorrelation by pair class,

00:10:26.206 --> 00:10:29.590
in the style of Smouse and Peakall, does the same along a distance axis.

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:32.430
Nor do you need to account separately for shared parents.

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:34.964
Kinship is already the sum of all shared ancestry,

00:10:34.964 --> 00:10:37.780
through every path, including shared parents and inbreeding loops.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:40.280
It is exactly the quantity this question needs.

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:44.060
Chapter four.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:49.210
Measuring inbreeding: what heterozygosity hides, and what runs of homozygosity reveal.

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:55.487
The classic summary compares observed heterozygosity

00:10:55.487 --> 00:11:01.540
with the heterozygosity expected under Hardy–Weinberg proportions: F_IS = 1 − H_O / H_E.

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:05.749
F_IS measures non-random mating relative to the current population:

00:11:05.749 --> 00:11:09.570
whether mates are more related than two random members of this generation.

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:11.490
That is not the same as being inbred.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:15.150
Watch a small, closed population that mates completely at random.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:20.651
Drift makes everyone slightly more related each generation,

00:11:20.651 --> 00:11:25.060
and inbreeding accumulates: F_t = 1 − (1 − 1/2N)^t.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:30.009
But because everyone is equally related, mates are no more related than random pairs,

00:11:30.009 --> 00:11:34.010
and observed heterozygosity keeps tracking its expectation, even as both decline.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:37.260
F_IS stays near zero while true inbreeding climbs.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:41.520
A bottlenecked population can be deeply inbred with an F_IS of zero.

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:44.472
Heterozygosity compared with today's expectation cannot

00:11:44.472 --> 00:11:46.700
see inbreeding that the whole population shares.

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:52.110
A heterozygote deficit can also be manufactured.

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:54.852
The first culprit is structure: pool two populations,

00:11:54.852 --> 00:11:57.900
and the Wahlund effect removes heterozygotes that were never missing.

00:11:58.650 --> 00:11:59.830
The second is genotyping.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:03.375
At low sequencing depth, one allele of a heterozygote may never be read,

00:12:03.375 --> 00:12:04.860
and the call becomes a homozygote.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:09.330
Allelic dropout and null alleles do the same, and with RADseq data they can dominate the signal.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:14.290
A useful diagnostic: plot each individual's heterozygosity against its mean depth.

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:17.870
If heterozygosity rises with depth, the apparent inbreeding is technical.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:22.270
Apply depth filters, or use methods that work with genotype likelihoods rather than hard calls.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:24.280
The third culprit is filtering.

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:28.661
Removing loci that depart from Hardy–Weinberg proportions preferentially discards those

00:12:28.661 --> 00:12:32.230
with the largest heterozygote deficits, biasing inbreeding estimates downward.

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:34.520
Filter within populations, and leniently.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.260
Minor allele frequency filters shift the estimates too.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.984
Individual estimates of F come in several flavors:

00:12:42.984 --> 00:12:47.040
F_hom from PLINK, and F_GRM and F_UNI from the GCTA software.

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.310
All are still relative to the sample's allele frequencies,

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:52.240
and all are noisy when markers are few.

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:57.830
Before trusting them, ask a prior question: does inbreeding vary among individuals at all?

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:02.325
If it does, an inbred individual tends to be homozygous across its whole genome,

00:13:02.325 --> 00:13:06.010
so heterozygosity at one set of loci predicts heterozygosity at another.

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.431
That correlation is called identity disequilibrium,

00:13:09.431 --> 00:13:13.830
and g2 measures it as the excess covariance in heterozygosity between pairs of loci.

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:16.451
A simpler view splits the loci into random halves

00:13:16.451 --> 00:13:19.170
and correlates each individual's heterozygosity across them.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:24.613
If g2 is indistinguishable from zero, differences in individual F are mostly sampling noise,

00:13:24.613 --> 00:13:28.030
and heterozygosity–fitness correlations built on them will be weak.

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:31.280
The inbreedR package estimates g2 with confidence intervals.

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:36.472
When an individual's parents share an ancestor,

00:13:36.472 --> 00:13:40.530
the individual can inherit the same chromosome segment twice, once through each parent.

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:45.630
The result is a long run of homozygosity: the physical footprint of autozygosity.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:48.980
The fraction of the genome inside such runs is F_ROH.

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:52.550
It depends far less on allele frequencies or a reference sample,

00:13:52.550 --> 00:13:57.850
so it largely sidesteps the base-population problem, and it captures the drift inbreeding that F_IS misses.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:03.350
A half-sibling mating leaves, on average, one eighth of the genome in runs, and those runs are long,

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:06.960
because only a few meioses separate the common ancestor from the individual.

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:09.290
Runs also carry a time stamp.

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:12.374
A common ancestor g generations back leaves runs

00:14:12.374 --> 00:14:15.450
with an expected length of about 100 / 2g centimorgans.

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:20.250
Recent inbreeding makes long runs; old background relatedness leaves many short ones.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.800
In wild populations, F_ROH has tracked realized inbreeding better than pedigree F,

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:28.460
because it measures what was actually inherited, not what the pedigree expects.

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:34.850
This is where the warnings in the literature on runs of homozygosity apply.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:38.310
They do not say that you must know relatedness before you look for runs.

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:40.970
Runs are how you infer how related the parents were.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:43.330
The first warning is about history.

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:45.796
In a population that has long been small,

00:14:45.796 --> 00:14:50.030
short runs from old shared ancestry are abundant, even without recent consanguinity.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:54.070
Partition F_ROH into length classes instead of reporting a single total.

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:56.040
The second is about calling.

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:59.150
PLINK's defaults were designed for dense human SNP arrays.

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:04.031
With sparse or uneven markers such as RADseq, genotyping errors fragment real runs,

00:15:04.031 --> 00:15:06.460
and poorly covered regions become false runs.

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:08.640
So choose the caller for the data.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:11.595
The bcftools roh command uses a hidden Markov model

00:15:11.595 --> 00:15:14.050
with genotype likelihoods and allele frequencies.

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:17.560
RZooRoH partitions autozygosity into age classes.

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:22.650
For low coverage, ngsF-HMM and ROHan work from genotype likelihoods.

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:26.870
PLINK is fast, once its window and density settings match your marker spacing.

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:31.019
Runs need a reference genome, ideally assembled to chromosomes,

00:15:31.019 --> 00:15:32.800
and dense, evenly spaced markers.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:37.520
High-coverage whole genomes are ideal, and low coverage works with likelihood-based callers.

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.020
RADseq is marginal.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:42.420
An amplicon panel of a few hundred SNPs cannot detect runs at all.

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:45.940
Chapter five.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:49.590
Choosing methods: matching the tool to your data and your question.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:55.180
Likelihood-based pedigree reconstruction turns the problem around.

00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:58.552
Instead of estimating kinship and then guessing relationships,

00:15:58.552 --> 00:16:02.290
it proposes a whole pedigree and asks how probable the genotypes are under it.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:04.630
Inbreeding is not a nuisance here.

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:08.621
It is simply a loop in the pedigree, and the likelihood accounts for it automatically,

00:16:08.621 --> 00:16:11.670
because the pedigree itself generates the pattern of identity by descent.

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:15.701
COLONY reconstructs sibships and parentage by maximum likelihood,

00:16:15.701 --> 00:16:17.670
with an explicit option for inbreeding.

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:21.349
The sequoia package is built for SNP panels of a few hundred loci,

00:16:21.349 --> 00:16:25.820
and uses birth years and age priors to separate relationships that genotypes alone cannot.

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:30.156
For pairwise questions, choose estimators that permit inbreeding:

00:16:30.156 --> 00:16:33.521
NgsRelate version two, which works from genotype likelihoods,

00:16:33.521 --> 00:16:38.210
or the coancestry function in the related package, with allow.inbreeding set to true.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:42.600
Put the pieces together as a map.

00:16:42.1000 --> 00:16:44.950
Across the top is the question you are asking.

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:46.820
Down the side is the data you have.

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:52.180
For mean kinship among classes of pairs, as in sex-biased dispersal, almost any data will do.

00:16:52.580 --> 00:16:55.050
Moment estimators or a genomic relationship matrix,

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:57.860
with permutation tests, work even with a few hundred SNPs.

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:01.699
For parentage and sibships, likelihood pedigree methods work from

00:17:01.699 --> 00:17:04.740
a few hundred well-chosen SNPs upward, especially with age data.

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:08.806
Separating half siblings from grandparents or aunts needs segments from dense

00:17:08.806 --> 00:17:12.330
markers on a chromosome-level assembly, or else ages and pedigree context.

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:14.344
For individual inbreeding,

00:17:14.344 --> 00:17:17.940
a sparse panel supports only noisy marker-based F, worth checking with g2.

00:17:18.340 --> 00:17:21.650
RADseq supports likelihood-based heterozygosity and cautious runs.

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:25.550
Whole genomes support F_ROH by length class, the most informative measure.

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:28.230
And whatever the cell, structure comes first.

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:33.300
If your sample spans genetic clusters, analyze within them, or use KING-robust and PC-Relate.

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:40.450
The real answer to how you can ever estimate either one reliably is simulation.

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:44.242
Take your own allele frequencies and your own marker set, simulate pedigrees

00:17:44.242 --> 00:17:48.080
with known relationships, including inbred ones, and see what your estimators recover.

00:17:48.830 --> 00:17:51.460
Each relationship class produces a distribution of estimates.

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:56.336
With many markers, unrelated pairs, half siblings and first-degree pairs separate cleanly,

00:17:56.336 --> 00:17:59.270
and k0 then splits parent and offspring from full siblings.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:02.540
With fewer markers, or more inbreeding, they overlap.

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:06.183
The overlap is the misclassification rate: the fraction of half siblings

00:18:06.183 --> 00:18:09.200
that look unrelated, or full siblings that look like half siblings.

00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:11.590
Tools exist for exactly this.

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:16.964
The related package simulates families, sequoia's EstConf estimates assignment error rates,

00:18:16.964 --> 00:18:21.500
and CKMRsim computes false positive and false negative rates for each kin category.

00:18:22.250 --> 00:18:26.307
If the simulations show that your panel cannot separate full from half siblings at plausible

00:18:26.307 --> 00:18:30.100
levels of inbreeding, do not rely on that distinction, no matter which method you use.

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:34.090
Return to the puzzle.

00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:37.880
Relatedness seemed to need inbreeding, and inbreeding seemed to need relatedness.

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:41.916
But both are identity by descent, between two individuals or within one,

00:18:41.916 --> 00:18:44.290
and they are estimated together, in one matrix.

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:47.165
The genuine dependency runs to the reference:

00:18:47.165 --> 00:18:50.990
the allele frequencies and population structure that define what unrelated means.

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:54.190
Model the structure, and the loop becomes an iteration that converges.

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:55.1000
Then follow the recipe.

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:58.330
Do quality control with inbreeding in mind.

00:18:58.730 --> 00:18:59.850
Deal with structure first.

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:01.600
Ask whether inbreeding varies.

00:19:01.1000 --> 00:19:03.150
Choose methods that allow it.

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:05.990
Use runs of homozygosity when your data can see them.

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:08.730
And validate everything by simulating your own system.

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:12.280
Relatedness and inbreeding are two views of one process:

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:14.980
shared ancestry, passed down chromosome by chromosome.

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:17.269
Measure it against a reference you understand,

00:19:17.269 --> 00:19:19.610
and the chicken and the egg turn out to be the same bird.
